  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the penal law, in relation to the crime of assault of an operator of a for-hire vehicle",
    "basePrintNo": "S5690",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5690-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§60.07 & 120.05, Pen L",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5690\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the penal law, in relation to the crime of assault of\nan operator of a for-hire vehicle\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF THE BILL :\n\nTo protect for-hire drivers, including taxi drivers, from being\nassaulted on the job by posting signs in taxis to alert passengers of\nthe punishment for assaulting taxi workers; and to include for-hire\ndrivers in the list of victims of felony assault.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 titles the bill the \"taxi driver protection act.\"\n\nSection 2 amends section 60.07 of the penal law to require every\nfor-hire vehicle to post a sign on the interior of such vehicle that\nstates \"ATTENTION: Assaulting a Taxi Driver Is Punishable By Up To\nTwenty-Five Years In Prison.\"\n\nSection 3 amends Subdivision 11 of section 120.05 of the penal law to\ninclude drivers of for-hire vehicles in the occupations listed under\nvictims of felony assault.\n\nSection 4 sets forth the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nAccording to the Occupational Safety and Health Administration, taxi\nworkers are thirty times more likely to be killed on the job than\nother workers. New York City is home to the largest taxi driver\nindustry in the country.\n\nPosting warning signs in taxi cabs would facilitate a public\ninformation campaign designed to inform the public that attacks on\ntaxi workers are a very serious offense. New York City Council has\nalready passed a bill that mandates these signs, so this legislation\nwould regulate such signage on a statewide basis; bringing better\nconsistency to the State's workers' protection.\n\nDue to recent targeted attacks against taxi and for-hire drivers, this\nbill would also include for-hire drivers as an occupation in the list\nof victims of felony assault in the Penal Law. Other occupations of\nsimilar customer base and skill sets are already included and\nprotected in the current list of victims, such as bus operators, train\noperators, station agents and station cleaners. It is important to\nallow for-hire drivers to be protected under this law, especially due\nto the increase in state-wide for-hire services.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n(2010) A.11704\n(2010) veto 6808 of 2010 A.10058\n(2012) A.207-B\n(2013-2014) Referred to Codes (Kim)\n(2015-2016) Referred to Codes (Kim) A.2431\n(2107-2018)\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nNone to the State.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately; provided however that section\nthree of this act shall take effect on the first of November next\nsucceeding the date on which it shall have become a law.\n",
